import RequestProject.Epistemic.Bridge

/-!
# Bounded accumulation and the commitment threshold

A curator does not act on every source it reads.  Sources are *accumulated*
into a bounded tally, and a separate *threshold* decides when the tally is
enough to act on.  The two are different objects, and this module keeps them
apart:

* accumulation is bounded — the tally saturates at the cap and never exceeds it
  (`tally_le_cap`, `accumulate_saturates`), and it is monotone
  (`le_accumulate`);
* out-of-scope sources do not accumulate at all (`accumulate_irrelevant`), so
  the same source read under two scopes gives two different tallies
  (`scope_changes_tally`);
* accumulating is not committing: a tally can rise while the decision stays
  open (`accumulation_is_not_commitment`), and commitment is monotone in the
  tally (`commit_mono`);
* **the threshold is not truth.**  Crossing it does not make the claim hold
  (`committed_does_not_entail_holds`), and a claim that holds does not have to
  be committed to (`holds_does_not_entail_committed`).  Both directions are
  exhibited against the executable Wikidata checker, so neither is an artefact
  of a missing definition.

The evidence *state* of a claim (`Epistemic.Trit`) is therefore not recoverable
from the commitment (`commitment_does_not_determine_state`): commitment is a
policy about acting, evidence is a claim about the world.
-/

